{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And his wife shall go out with him</b> Rashi writes (Rashi on Exodus 21:3): \"Now who brought her in that she should go out? Rather, this teaches that one who acquires a Hebrew slave is obligated to provide food for his wife and children.\" This is a midrash of the Sages (Mekhilta here). And they included the children along with her, as derived from what is written afterward: \"then he shall go out from you, he and his children with him\" (Leviticus 25:41). And it has not become clear to me regarding this law whether the labor of the wife and children belongs to the master for all the days that he sustains them. And what appears correct in my eyes is that he enters in place of the husband, for the Torah had compassion on the wife and children whose lives hang upon them, looking expectantly toward the hands of the husband, and now that he has been sold they would perish in their distress. Therefore He commanded the master, who is taking the labor of his hands, to be in his place for them. And if so, the obligation upon the master should be only the husband's obligation alone — he takes the labor of their hands just as the husband would do, and sustains and provides for them. And this is the meaning of the phrase \"and his wife shall go out with him,\" for the slave's wife was with her husband like a maidservant to his master, since the labor of both of them belongs to him and he is obligated for their food — the only difference between them is that the woman has the prerogative to leave on her own. And likewise the children: he is not obligated for their food except for their upkeep in the time when the father is commanded or customarily sustains them. And so Rashi explained in tractate Kiddushin (22a, s.v. דלאו).<br>And all this is compassion from HaShem upon them and upon the slave, so that he not die in his distress while his toil is in a stranger's house, and his children and wife would be abandoned — even though he was not obligated for their food by Torah law, as is explained in the Talmud in Ketuvot (49a). But since it is the way of the whole world for a man to support his wife and young children, the El in His mercy commanded that the one who acquires him be like a compassionate father to them. And the intent of the Sages regarding \"his children\" is sons and daughters. And I found in the Mekhilta (here): \"One might think he is obligated to provide food for his betrothed (arusah) and for a yavam's waiting widow (shomeret yavam) — Scripture says 'his wife,' to exclude the shomeret yavam, who is not his wife; 'with him,' to exclude the arusah, who is not with him.\" And this too is a proof for the law that I stated, for an arusah and a shomeret yavam do not depend on the man for support, and therefore the Torah did not impose their food upon the master. And even if the yavam and the arus are obligated by law to sustain them during the known period discussed in the Talmud (Ketuvot 57b), that is upon him like the rest of his obligations, and the master is not obligated for them. Furthermore, I found in the Mekhilta, the latter version of Rabbi Shimon: \"'If he was married to a woman' — just as he is obligated for his own food, so too he is obligated for the food of his wife and children. I would still say this applies to a wife and children he had before his master acquired him, that he is obligated for their food, for initially he acquired him only on that condition; but a wife and children he had after his master acquired him, he should not be obligated for their food — Scripture says 'if he was married to a woman,' etc. — two wives are mentioned here: one before his master acquired him and one after his master acquired him. One might think that even if he had an arusah and a shomeret yavam for whom he is not obligated to provide food, his master would be obligated for their food — 'his wife and his children' prove that he is not obligated for their food, yet his master would be obligated for theirs; Scripture says 'and his wife shall go out with him' — a wife who is with him, the master is obligated; this one who is not with him is excluded. One might think even one who is not fit to remain with him, such as a widow to a High Priest, a divorcee and a halutzah to an ordinary priest — Scripture says 'and his wife shall go out with him' — one who is fit to remain with him is included; this one is excluded, etc. One might think even if he married a woman without his master's knowledge — Scripture says 'he' — just as 'he' means with his master's knowledge, so too his wife means with his master's knowledge. One might think the labor of his sons and daughters would belong to the master — and it is a logical inference (kal va-chomer): if a Canaanite slave, whose master is not obligated to sustain him, still the labor of his sons and daughters belongs to the master, then a Hebrew slave, whose master is obligated to sustain him — is it not logical that the labor of his sons and daughters should belong to the master? Scripture says 'he' — his own hands' labor belongs to the master, but the labor of his sons and daughters does not belong to the master. 'And his wife shall go out with him' — do not separate him from his wife; do not separate him from his children.\" Until here is this baraita. And I still say that if his wife and children wished to be sustained at the master's expense, he takes the labor of their hands as I have written, and they came in this baraita only to teach that they are not his by the law of a Canaanite slave, nor by the law of the slave himself, but rather they are able to say: we will not be sustained and we will not work. And what is newly derived for us from this baraita is that if he married a woman without his master's consent, he is not obligated for her food nor for the food of her children, since it is within his master's prerogative to give him a Canaanite maidservant, and he is not obligated for the food of this Israelite woman. And they further expounded from the word \"with him\" — do not separate him from his wife and from his children — to say that his master cannot say to him: with the maidservant I gave you, you shall be secluded and sleep at night, and not with your Israelite wife. Rather, the matter is in the slave's discretion."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The Rashi reference in Kiddushin appears to be to 22a in standard pagination; Ramban cites it as כב. (22). The Ketuvot references (מט. and נז.) are resolved to 49a and 57b respectively by standard tractate pagination. The Mekhilta passage attributed to 'the latter version of Rabbi Shimon' (אחריתא דרבי שמעון) appears to distinguish between two textual witnesses or two versions of the midrash."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:17:58.743881043Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}